From 3c84df55abd0cd603cb6591a13904ba5b2d0f2c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jared Wyles <jared.wyles@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 21:27:50 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 23/32] llama: keep a sequence's unified-pool cells in chunks

The unified pool placed a ubatch's tokens in consecutive cells, so at four
concurrent requests each sequence's decode cells sat at stride four and its
row list held one run per token. A token now goes to the cell after its
sequence's highest one while that stays inside the same 64-cell chunk and
free, else to the first cell of a fresh all-empty chunk; a pool too
fragmented for a fresh chunk falls back to any empty cell. Row lists hold
long runs the row-attention kernels read at dense speed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01QA3dWey3RBtBgDz1Tery9Y
